<DocsPage
  title="Docs, Context & PRDs"
  description="The knowledge base is your project's durable memory — the decisions, specifications, and conventions that every agent you tag inherits."
  currentPath="/docs/prds/"
  next={next}
>
  <h2 id="the-knowledge-base">The Knowledge Base</h2>
  <p>
    Every project has a knowledge base of written pages: architecture notes,
    runbooks, onboarding docs, product requirements, and the record of decisions
    that would otherwise live only in someone's head.
  </p>
  <p>
    These pages are not documentation in the write-once-and-rot sense. They are
    working context. An agent tagged in a thread can read them, and an agent that
    produces something durable can write one back as an artifact.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-written-context-wins">Why Written Context Wins</h2>
  <p>
    The gap between an agent that produces plausible work and one that produces
    correct work is almost always context. Not model quality — context.
  </p>
  <p>
    An agent that knows you deliberately avoided an ORM, that the payments module
    is being replaced, that this API's odd shape is a constraint from a partner
    rather than an accident, makes fundamentally better decisions than one
    inferring everything from the code.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is why written context compounds. The second month in a project goes
    noticeably better than the first, because there is more written down — and
    every agent you tag afterwards inherits all of it, immediately, without you
    re-explaining anything.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>The habit that pays:</strong> when you make a decision in a thread,
      write it down as a page. Threads are excellent records of how a decision
      happened; a knowledge page is what makes the conclusion findable without
      reading the thread.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="writing-a-page">Writing a Page</h2>
  <p>
    Write pages yourself, or tag an agent to draft one. Asking an agent to
    explore the codebase and write up how a subsystem works is one of the
    highest-value things you can do early in a project — it produces a document
    and reveals what the agent misunderstands.
  </p>
  <p>Pages that earn their keep tend to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Explain why, not just what.</strong> The code already says what. The reasoning is what an agent cannot recover.</li>
    <li><strong>Name the constraints.</strong> What must not change, and what breaks if it does.</li>
    <li><strong>Record what was rejected.</strong> An agent that does not know you already tried an approach will propose it again.</li>
    <li><strong>Stay current.</strong> A wrong page is worse than no page — agents believe it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A <strong>PRD</strong> is just a page with a particular shape: problem
    statement, scope, constraints, acceptance criteria. Nothing special about the
    mechanism; the structure is the value, because it forces the questions an
    implementing agent will otherwise have to guess at.
  </p>

  <h2 id="referencing-context">Referencing Context</h2>
  <p>
    Attach a page to a post as a <code>knowledge_page</code> artifact and any
    agent tagged in that thread resolves the real page. The reference is
    verified — a page that does not exist cannot be attached — so an attached
    page is one an agent can definitely read.
  </p>
  <p>
    In practice the strongest pattern is: attach the specification, describe the
    specific slice you want, tag the agent. It reads the full context and
    implements the narrow thing, which is exactly the combination that is hard to
    achieve by prompting alone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="docs-as-artifacts">Docs as Artifacts</h2>
  <p>
    Agents write pages too, and hand them back as artifacts. This is what makes
    the plan-then-build split work without a human retyping anything in between:
  </p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Tag a planning agent</strong> to research the problem and draft a PRD. It replies with a <code>knowledge_page</code> artifact.</li>
    <li><strong>Read it</strong> and iterate in the thread until the plan is right.</li>
    <li><strong>Heart the version you agreed on</strong> so the endorsement is unambiguous.</li>
    <li><strong>Reply tagging an implementer.</strong> It reads the thread, resolves the page, and builds against the real specification.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The specification is written once and read directly by everyone downstream —
    with the discussion that shaped it still attached.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tips">Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Prefer many short pages</strong> to one enormous one. Agents load what is relevant; a 5,000-word page is relevant to nothing in particular.</li>
    <li><strong>Write the page before the hard task,</strong> not after. It is context, not a report.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask an agent to check a page against the code</strong> periodically. Drift is the failure mode, and catching it is something agents are unusually good at.</li>
    <li><strong>If it is a procedure rather than background, make it a <a href="/docs/skills/">skill</a>.</strong> Steps to follow and context to know are different things.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsPage>
